WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.237 --> 00:00:03.363
- [Narrator] This is
Taries — a Ring-Tailed Lemur.

00:00:03.363 --> 00:00:05.530
He is quite the character.

00:00:07.826 --> 00:00:10.818
Of the 100 or so species
of lemurs in Madagascar, 

00:00:10.818 --> 00:00:13.382
the Ring-Tailed Lemur is
easily the most iconic

00:00:13.382 --> 00:00:17.721
because of its black
and white ringed tail.

00:00:17.721 --> 00:00:20.805
They're mostly vegetarian
feeding on fruit, leaves,

00:00:20.805 --> 00:00:24.012
flowers, bark and the
occasional insect or lizard

00:00:24.012 --> 00:00:26.450
that they find in the
desert scrubland and woods

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:28.700
of southwestern Madagascar.

00:00:30.139 --> 00:00:31.997
They're more social than most lemurs

00:00:31.997 --> 00:00:35.936
with as many as 24 members in a group.

00:00:35.936 --> 00:00:37.869
When traveling together
they keep their tails

00:00:37.869 --> 00:00:40.724
raised in the air like
flags in order to make sure

00:00:40.724 --> 00:00:42.141
no one gets lost.

00:00:43.645 --> 00:00:46.084
Unfortunately, they have to travel farther

00:00:46.084 --> 00:00:47.996
to find suitable habitats.

00:00:47.996 --> 00:00:50.042
The habitats they prefer to call home

00:00:50.042 --> 00:00:51.907
are being rapidly destroyed

00:00:51.907 --> 00:00:55.126
by expanding farmland,
overgrazing from livestock,

00:00:55.126 --> 00:00:57.459
and charcoal harvesting.

00:00:57.459 --> 00:01:00.283
While conservation has battled
for the lemurs survival,

00:01:00.283 --> 00:01:01.704
there's hope.

00:01:01.704 --> 00:01:04.712
Ring-Tailed Lemurs breed very
successfully in captivity

00:01:04.712 --> 00:01:07.204
and there are over 1,000 individuals

00:01:07.204 --> 00:01:09.621
at 140 zoos around the world.

00:01:10.612 --> 00:01:13.112
This is the Ring-Tailed Lemur.

00:01:14.063 --> 00:01:17.146
(steady chime music)

